WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.779
The Star Trek franchise returned to the silver
screen with its highly successful 2009 reboot

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:10.160
of the same name, but a decade later, the
whole series seems to be stuck in limbo.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.580
But just how did it all go so wrong, so fast?

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.440
Part of the beauty of the Star Trek universe
is how many different takes, visions, and

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:22.200
ideas fans are able to see applied to the
classic Wagon-Train-in-space formula.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.020
Over the decades, many different people have
taken the helm, each with their own style

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.700
of cinematography, concept of characterization,
and visual flair.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.580
Some love J.J. Abrams' slick take on the Enterprise
and her inhabitants, while some prefer Gene

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:38.840
Roddenberry's original work, and others are
Deep Space Nine diehards.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.560
And the wonderful thing is that they can actually
coexist.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:45.200
Everyone has their favorite director, just
like everyone has their favorite captain.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.140
But there is a downside to this directorial
shuffling, and it's part of the reason the

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.420
films have run aground in the way that they
have.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.960
Star Trek 4 has changed hands more than once:
Quentin Tarantino and S.J.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:01.190
Clarkson were both rumored as directors at
various times, then a confusing pseudo-clarification

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:06.220
was made stating that Clarkson's movie would
be separate and released before Tarantino's.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:09.820
Multiple writers and producers were named,
changed, and named again.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:14.660
No vision was ever truly able to coalesce
between any of these creators, and the fans

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:16.700
had nothing to center their excitement around.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.620
"I can see no appeal in either option."

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.420
"Well believe it or not, neither can I."

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:25.200
Now the project has fallen apart entirely,
and no one is even really certain what might

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.500
have been, since the film was never able to
settle around any one team or vision.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:34.000
It just proves the old adage right: you don't
want too many captains on the bridge.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:39.900
2009's Star Trek was a box office smash as
well as a critical darling, garnering a solid

00:01:39.909 --> 00:01:43.180
little fandom of its own, full of fans who
were more than willing to see more of the

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:44.840
Enterprise's new adventures.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:50.079
The sequel, Into Darkness, was unable to recreate
the success of the first film, and constituted

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:53.440
a step down for the franchise both critically
and commercially.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.000
But it wasn't a real failure, and so the Hollywood
wheels kept turning, and Star Trek Beyond

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:58.840
was released.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:03.160
This film was a further step downwards, since
it underperformed at the box office and ended

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.340
up lost in the midst of its competitors.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:09.560
The writing was on the wall: Star Trek was
traveling a downward trajectory that showed

00:02:09.569 --> 00:02:12.030
no signs of changing course any time soon.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:16.099
A movie as great as that first Star Trek could
turn anything around, of course, but great

00:02:16.099 --> 00:02:20.269
movies aren't easy to make, and evidently
the executives who hold the purse strings

00:02:20.269 --> 00:02:21.760
didn't want to take a risk.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:25.720
Star Trek 4 was a gambit, and nobody could
guarantee that it would pay off.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:31.160
One thing that made the 2000s Trek movies
the successes they (mostly) were was the cast.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:36.079
Zachary Quinto as Spock brought new warmth
to the famously chilly Vulcan, Zoe Saldana

00:02:36.079 --> 00:02:40.650
was a quippy wonder as Lt. Uhura, and even
the smaller characters proved to be nothing

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:41.920
less than outstanding.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.280
People love Star Trek for a lot of reasons,
but there's a reason the debate always comes

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:50.840
down to favorite captains: because fans are
ultimately in it for the characters.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:55.340
There's no bigger personality on the Enterprise
than Captain Kirk, of course, and Chris Pine

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.140
proved he could deliver an irresistible take
on the role.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.620
Pine's charisma lit up the screen and became
a linchpin of the film's success, but that

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:05.440
ended up being a curse as much as it was a
blessing.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:10.920
Star Trek 4 endured months of rumors about
casting turmoil, and in August 2018, it was

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:14.600
revealed that talks to secure Pine for the
project had fallen through.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:18.500
There's no Star Trek without Kirk, and no
Kirk without Chris Pine.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.960
So now there's no Star Trek at all.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:26.880
Back in 2009, Star Trek was in theaters, blowing
minds with its unique brand of brash, sassy

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:27.900
science fiction adventure.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.879
But back then, the world wasn't used to that
sort of thing.

00:03:30.879 --> 00:03:34.860
Iron Man was only a year old, and the scale
and depth of the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:38.720
as it exists today was still just a twinkle
in Kevin Feige's eye.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.660
The idea of a talking raccoon with a tragic
backstory and a fondness for oversize weaponry

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:45.260
was practically laughable.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.560
And no one even knew who Kylo Ren was yet.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:52.310
Geek had yet to become chic, and Star Trek
was pretty much the only game in town.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:57.219
Nowadays, however, everyone knows who Rocket
Raccoon is, and Saturday Night Live is making

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:58.700
jokes about vibranium.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:00.060
"It's locked!

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:03.300
Maybe if you had some vibranium it would open."

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:03.980
"Say what?"

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:08.740
Genre fiction, once maligned, now rules the
world, and Star Trek has been outstripped

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:12.680
by Marvel, Star Wars, and all kinds of other
sci-fi/fantasy titans.

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:17.280
Though the Star Trek sequels were hardly flops,
the reality is, they weren't the smash hits

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:21.480
they needed to be to keep up with the likes
of The Avengers and The Force Awakens.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:26.460
To be merely passable ended up being an unforgivable
sin in the eyes of the public…and now we're

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:29.660
all terrified of the First Order instead of
Klingons.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:35.000
Star Trek is a franchise that spans many mediums,
but its roots are in television, and likely

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:36.100
always will be.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:41.820
In 2017, the franchise returned to its roots
with Star Trek: Discovery on the CBS All Access

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:42.840
streaming service.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:47.291
Discovery begins roughly one decade before
the events of the original Star Trek, and

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:51.400
follows the adventures of the crew of the
USS Discovery, led by anthropologist Michael

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:52.300
Burnham.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:57.440
Though its run so far has been hamstrung by
what many see as a slow start and some wonky

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.180
writing, reviews have trended upwards and
viewership has been decent.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:03.820
Unfortunately, decent just isn't enough.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.880
The truth is that a massively popular new
installment of the series could have injected

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:11.570
the movies with the adrenaline they needed
to get out of development hell, but the numbers

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:13.000
simply aren't there.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.540
Discovery is solid, but not a smash.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:19.910
Good for fans of Star Trek as a streaming
serial, but bad news for those who want more

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:22.860
big screen adventures for the Enterprise and
her crew.

